WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.647 --> 00:00:02.480
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Hi
I'm Priyanka Vergadia,

00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:04.910
and this is AI
Simplified where we

00:00:04.910 --> 00:00:07.640
learn to make your data useful.

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:09.410
In the previous
episodes, we learned

00:00:09.410 --> 00:00:13.130
how to create data sets in
Vertex AI and trained machine

00:00:13.130 --> 00:00:15.290
learning model using auto ML.

00:00:15.290 --> 00:00:18.620
In this video, we help
our friends at Fuel Symbol

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:21.170
build custom machine
learning models.

00:00:21.170 --> 00:00:23.420
Their team of machine
learning experts

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:25.490
have decided to write
their own training

00:00:25.490 --> 00:00:29.030
code to predict the fuel
efficiency of a vehicle.

00:00:29.030 --> 00:00:32.600
Join me as I walk them through
the different ways in which you

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:35.450
can run custom
training in Vertex AI.

00:00:35.450 --> 00:00:39.110
And finally, we will kick off
our training job in the console

00:00:39.110 --> 00:00:40.940
and see how it's done.

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:44.510
Now what do we need to
create a custom job?

00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:47.660
Before we submit any of the
custom jobs to Vertex AI,

00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:51.260
we need to create a Python
training application or custom

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:55.430
container to write the training
code and the dependencies.

00:00:55.430 --> 00:00:58.310
There are pre-built
containers to run our code

00:00:58.310 --> 00:01:01.340
if we write our training
application in Python

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:06.110
using TensorFlow, scikit-learn,
XG Boost or PyTorch.

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:08.720
If you are using
pre-built containers,

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:10.580
then you can just
provide the path

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:13.670
to the training package
in Cloud storage.

00:01:13.670 --> 00:01:17.000
You can also build your
own custom containers,

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:18.080
if you wish to.

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:20.780
And in that case, you
provide a location

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:23.330
to the container from
your container registry

00:01:23.330 --> 00:01:25.910
or your artifact registry.

00:01:25.910 --> 00:01:29.180
If you're not sure whether
to use pre-built or custom

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:32.150
containers, then check out
the link I've included below.

00:01:32.150 --> 00:01:36.230
In both cases, you would need
a folder within Cloud storage

00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:40.340
bucket to put your model
output artifacts into.

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:43.250
When running custom
training jobs on Vertex AI,

00:01:43.250 --> 00:01:48.440
we can also make use of Vertex's
hyperparameter tuning service.

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:50.600
If you're not familiar
with hyperparameters,

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:53.480
they are variables that
govern the process of training

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:57.350
your models, such as batch
size or number of hidden layers

00:01:57.350 --> 00:01:58.970
in a deep neural network.

00:01:58.970 --> 00:02:01.760
In hyperparameter tuning
jobs, the Vertex AI

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:04.250
creates trials of
your training job

00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:06.620
with different sets
of hyperparameters,

00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:10.250
and then searches for the best
combination of hyperparameters

00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:13.380
across a series of those trials.

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:17.120
Now to run the training,
you need compute resources.

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:20.510
It can be either single
node or multiple worker

00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:22.640
pools for distributed training.

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:24.530
This is also the
step where you would

00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:27.980
select the type of
machines, the CPUs, the disk

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:32.300
size, and the disk type, and
the accelerators, such as GPUs.

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:34.610
And once that
training is done, we

00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:37.400
need the trained
module as an endpoint

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:39.320
to be served for predictions.

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:44.180
In Vertex AI, we can do that by
using pre-built containers that

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:47.840
are supported runtime such
as TensorFlow, scikit-learn,

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:48.920
PyTorch.

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:52.010
Or you can also build
your own custom container

00:02:52.010 --> 00:02:56.240
that is stored in container
registry or artifact registry.

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:58.760
Of course, if you just
need the module artifacts

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:02.840
to be downloaded, then you can
skip these steps altogether.

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:06.200
Or you can just use the
model for batch predictions.

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:09.170
Now that we have the basic
understanding of our custom

00:03:09.170 --> 00:03:11.930
training choices,
let's help Fuel Symbol

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:15.410
train and sell a custom
model that predicts the fuel

00:03:15.410 --> 00:03:17.570
efficiency of a vehicle.

00:03:17.570 --> 00:03:20.060
We will be using TensorFlow
to build this model.

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:21.800
And then we'll package
our training code

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:22.920
in a Docker container.

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:24.080
So let's see.

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:27.090
In the Google Cloud
console, before we begin,

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:30.830
make sure to enable the
Vertex AI API Compute Engine

00:03:30.830 --> 00:03:32.870
and Container Registry APIs.

00:03:32.870 --> 00:03:34.970
In this example, we
will use Notebooks

00:03:34.970 --> 00:03:37.490
to create the code for
our Docker container.

00:03:37.490 --> 00:03:40.880
But you can use any environment
you like to do this.

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:44.510
Create a new notebook instance
with the latest version

00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:46.640
of TensorFlow Enterprise.

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:50.120
For this particular demo,
I am training without GPUs.

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:54.590
But depending on your use case,
choose GPUs if you need them.

00:03:54.590 --> 00:03:56.570
Now that our
instance is created,

00:03:56.570 --> 00:04:00.110
let's open the JupyterLab
and navigate to the terminal

00:04:00.110 --> 00:04:02.090
window in our notebook.

00:04:02.090 --> 00:04:05.600
For the purpose of this demo,
we will use the training code

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:08.150
that already exists
in TensorFlow Docs

00:04:08.150 --> 00:04:11.720
to predict the fuel
efficiency of a vehicle.

00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:16.310
It uses the auto MPG data
set from Kaggle to predict,

00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:20.240
and I've included the
link below to this page.

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:23.630
What we will do here is
we submit the training job

00:04:23.630 --> 00:04:27.140
to Vertex AI by putting our
training called in a Docker

00:04:27.140 --> 00:04:30.860
container and pushing the
container to Google Container

00:04:30.860 --> 00:04:31.880
Registry.

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:34.430
Using this approach,
we can train a model

00:04:34.430 --> 00:04:36.770
built with any framework.

00:04:36.770 --> 00:04:40.670
Now, our first step is
to create a Docker file.

00:04:40.670 --> 00:04:44.750
This Docker file uses one of
the deep learning container

00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:47.780
TensorFlow Enterprise
Docker images.

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:50.630
Now the deep learning
containers on Google Cloud

00:04:50.630 --> 00:04:53.780
come with many common machine
learning and data science

00:04:53.780 --> 00:04:55.700
frameworks preinstalled.

00:04:55.700 --> 00:04:58.940
The one we're using here
includes TensorFlow Enterprise,

00:04:58.940 --> 00:05:02.210
Pandas, scikit-learn,
and others.

00:05:02.210 --> 00:05:04.400
Now after downloading
that image,

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:07.100
this Docker file sets
up the entry point

00:05:07.100 --> 00:05:08.270
for our training code.

00:05:08.270 --> 00:05:11.910
And we haven't created
these files yet.

00:05:11.910 --> 00:05:13.550
But we will do that now.

00:05:13.550 --> 00:05:16.820
Before that, we need
a Cloud Storage bucket

00:05:16.820 --> 00:05:20.570
to export our trained
TensorFlow model into.

00:05:20.570 --> 00:05:23.300
Vertex AI will then
use this bucket

00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:26.360
to read our imported
model assets,

00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:29.330
and to deploy the model.

00:05:29.330 --> 00:05:33.470
Now for our model code, let's
create a trainer directory

00:05:33.470 --> 00:05:35.960
and a train.py file.

00:05:35.960 --> 00:05:40.160
We would add our training
code to the train.py file.

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:43.040
Here, I have adapted the
code from the TensorFlow

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:46.250
docks for the model to
predict fuel efficiency

00:05:46.250 --> 00:05:47.780
for the vehicles.

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:50.360
Now if you're interested
in using the same code,

00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:53.030
follow the codelab
that's linked below.

00:05:53.030 --> 00:05:55.670
We need to make sure
we update the bucket

00:05:55.670 --> 00:05:58.250
variable with the name
of the storage bucket

00:05:58.250 --> 00:06:00.780
that we just created earlier.

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:04.790
Now we need to build and
test the container locally.

00:06:04.790 --> 00:06:06.860
Let's run the container
within our notebook

00:06:06.860 --> 00:06:09.630
to see if it is
working correctly.

00:06:09.630 --> 00:06:13.550
Now, it's building now.

00:06:13.550 --> 00:06:16.820
And now that it is built,
let's run this container.

00:06:19.770 --> 00:06:21.540
There it is.

00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:23.400
Working fine.

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:25.410
In a production
environment, training

00:06:25.410 --> 00:06:27.900
will likely take longer,
which is why you would

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:29.640
want to run it on the Cloud.

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:31.590
Now let's see how to do that.

00:06:31.590 --> 00:06:34.450
Now we push it to Google
Container Registry

00:06:34.450 --> 00:06:37.050
so Vertex AI can access it.

00:06:37.050 --> 00:06:40.510
With our container pushed
to Container Registry,

00:06:40.510 --> 00:06:45.150
we are now ready to kick off
a custom model training job.

00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:47.940
Now we go to the
training in Vertex AI

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:51.870
and select No managed
data sets since a data

00:06:51.870 --> 00:06:55.230
set is being pulled from the
source in our model code.

00:06:55.230 --> 00:06:57.390
Now Vertex AI gives
us two options

00:06:57.390 --> 00:06:58.710
for training the models.

00:06:58.710 --> 00:07:01.800
With auto ML, we can
train high quality models

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:04.500
with minimal effort and
machine learning expertise.

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:07.110
And we covered this in
our previous episodes.

00:07:07.110 --> 00:07:08.350
So check them out.

00:07:08.350 --> 00:07:11.670
Now here, we are
using custom training.

00:07:11.670 --> 00:07:14.670
Give the model a name and
in container settings,

00:07:14.670 --> 00:07:16.410
we have two options.

00:07:16.410 --> 00:07:20.430
Now pre-built containers
support PyTorch, scikit-learn,

00:07:20.430 --> 00:07:22.650
TensorFlow and XG Boost.

00:07:22.650 --> 00:07:25.230
It requires us to
package and upload

00:07:25.230 --> 00:07:29.370
our application code and
dependencies to a Cloud storage

00:07:29.370 --> 00:07:30.240
bucket.

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:33.150
Now because we have our
own custom container

00:07:33.150 --> 00:07:37.530
on Google Container Registry,
we will select Custom Container

00:07:37.530 --> 00:07:40.440
here, which supports not just
the common machine learning

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:43.920
frameworks, but also non
machine learning dependencies,

00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:46.470
libraries, and binaries.

00:07:46.470 --> 00:07:51.600
Now we provide our image URL
and our Cloud storage bucket.

00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:54.840
And we will see hyperparameter
tuning in later episodes.

00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:58.470
So let's leave this unchecked
for now and continue.

00:07:58.470 --> 00:08:02.730
In Compute and Pricing, we
set up a standard machine

00:08:02.730 --> 00:08:05.100
since this model trains quickly.

00:08:05.100 --> 00:08:08.340
But you're welcome to experiment
with larger machine types

00:08:08.340 --> 00:08:09.930
and GPUs if you like.

00:08:09.930 --> 00:08:12.930
Just remember that
if you use the GPUs,

00:08:12.930 --> 00:08:18.210
you will need to use a GPU
enabled base container image.

00:08:18.210 --> 00:08:20.460
The Prediction
Container section is

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:24.120
where we specify how we would
like to serve our model.

00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:26.520
Now here we use
pre-built containers.

00:08:26.520 --> 00:08:30.450
And we provide the path to
our Cloud storage bucket.

00:08:30.450 --> 00:08:33.960
And, we're ready for training.

00:08:33.960 --> 00:08:36.840
We can check to see the
training in progress.

00:08:36.840 --> 00:08:38.370
This could take some time.

00:08:38.370 --> 00:08:41.100
I have fast forwarded
the process here for you.

00:08:41.100 --> 00:08:43.470
Now that our model
is trained, we

00:08:43.470 --> 00:08:47.370
need to deploy it to an
endpoint to make predictions.

00:08:47.370 --> 00:08:49.990
Now give it a name and
since this is a demo,

00:08:49.990 --> 00:08:53.190
we will just leave the
traffic split at 100%,

00:08:53.190 --> 00:08:57.730
and enter 1 for minimum
number of compute nodes.

00:08:57.730 --> 00:09:00.840
Depending on the use case,
you can set the maximum number

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:03.840
of nodes for autoscaling when
the traffic on the endpoint

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:05.160
is high.

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:07.470
I'm selecting a standard
machine type here.

00:09:07.470 --> 00:09:11.280
Pick the one that makes sense
for your use case, and deploy.

00:09:11.280 --> 00:09:14.560
Deploying the endpoint
will take a few minutes.

00:09:14.560 --> 00:09:19.710
And when it is done, we can
see it in our Endpoint tab.

00:09:19.710 --> 00:09:21.630
Now that our
endpoint is deployed,

00:09:21.630 --> 00:09:23.500
we can start making predictions.

00:09:23.500 --> 00:09:25.350
We will get predictions
on our trained

00:09:25.350 --> 00:09:30.640
model from a Python notebook
using the Vertex AI Python SDK.

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:34.020
But you can get predictions
from any environment you like.

00:09:34.020 --> 00:09:36.330
So I'm back in our
notebook instance

00:09:36.330 --> 00:09:40.200
and creating a Python 3
notebook from the launcher.

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:44.010
Now we install
the Vertex AI SDK.

00:09:44.010 --> 00:09:47.310
Then add a cell
to import the SDK

00:09:47.310 --> 00:09:52.020
and create a reference to the
endpoint we just deployed.

00:09:52.020 --> 00:09:53.880
Replace the endpoint
and the project

00:09:53.880 --> 00:09:58.380
ID with our values of the
endpoint and project ID.

00:09:58.380 --> 00:10:00.720
And then finally,
we make a prediction

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:05.070
to our endpoint
with some test data.

00:10:05.070 --> 00:10:07.650
And there's our predicted value.

00:10:07.650 --> 00:10:10.800
With that, we've helped
our friends at Fuel Symbol

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:13.740
train a custom model
using Vertex AI.

00:10:13.740 --> 00:10:16.650
We provided the training
code in custom container,

00:10:16.650 --> 00:10:18.720
and used TensorFlow model here.

00:10:18.720 --> 00:10:22.260
But you can train a model
built with any framework using

00:10:22.260 --> 00:10:24.290
custom containers.

00:10:24.290 --> 00:10:26.300
Then we deployed
the TensorFlow model

00:10:26.300 --> 00:10:28.130
using pre-built containers.

00:10:28.130 --> 00:10:31.160
And we finally created
a model endpoint

00:10:31.160 --> 00:10:33.140
and generated a prediction.

00:10:33.140 --> 00:10:37.250
In the next video, we will build
a vision model using auto ML.

00:10:37.250 --> 00:10:39.350
In the meantime, let's
continue our discussion

00:10:39.350 --> 00:10:40.530
in the comments below.

00:10:40.530 --> 00:10:43.540
And don't forget to
like and subscribe.

